\description{
Read a MetaMorpheus AllQuantifiedProteinGroups.tsv as one row per protein group per sample group, with each sample group's spectral count and intensity as columns.

MetaMorpheus writes \code{AllQuantifiedProteinGroups.tsv} wide: one intensity column and one
spectral-count column per sample group. This reads it long - one row per protein group per
sample group - so the per-sample values are ordinary columns and nothing arrives as a map.
}
\details{
\strong{The table is unfiltered}, as MetaMorpheus writes it: decoys, contaminants and groups above 1\%
FDR are all rows. Filter on \code{q_value} and \code{decoy_contaminant_target} before you count anything.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_protein_group_records}. \code{record_count} counts the protein groups in the whole
  file; \code{returned_count} the groups returned, starting \code{offset} groups in; \code{row_count} the rows
  in \code{records}, which is groups times sample groups. \code{sample_labels} are the file's sample-group
  labels in header order, verbatim.

  \code{records} has one row per group per sample group: \code{q_value} a fraction from 0 to 1;
  \code{spectral_count} in PSMs; and \code{intensity} in the instrument's intensity units, \code{NA} where the
  cell was blank - not quantified in that sample group, which is not zero. \code{gene} and
  \code{organism} are \code{NA} where the file has no such column, and \code{absent_fields} then names them.
  \code{rows_not_read} is \code{NA}: every line is a group, or the file fails to read.

}
\section{Sample labels are verbatim}{


A label such as \code{QE-002106_GM1_a-calib} is what MetaMorpheus wrote in the header. Condition,
replicate and channel cannot be recovered from it reliably, so none is parsed out: map the
labels to your design yourself.

}
\section{The occupancy columns are elsewhere}{


MetaMorpheus's per-sample PTM occupancy cells have no column shape and are listed in
\code{excluded_fields}, whose \code{verb} column names \code{readers read-occupancy}: use
\code{\link{readers_read_occupancy}}, which reads them one row per site.

}
